sec.p:
Generative models provide probabilistic outcomes. This is assumed to account for the effects of countless unmodelled ‘large-world’ factors zt, which collectively make the future unpredictable from the perspective of the learner. Specifically, we could specify ground-truth models of state update and observation as xt = f (xt−1, zt), and yt = g(xt, zt). Probabilistic inference assumes that
(1a)
f(xt−1,zt)∼Px(xt∣xt−1,θ) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f\left(x_{t-1}, z_{t}\right) \sim P_{x}\left(x_{t} \mid x_{t-1}, \theta\right) \tag{(1a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(1b)
g(xt,zt)∼Py(yt∣xt,θ), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g\left(x_{t}, z_{t}\right) \sim P_{y}\left(y_{t} \mid x_{t}, \theta\right), \tag{1b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the distributions Px and Py are known ahead of time as part of the generative model, and the parameters θ are learnt during the task.

We first propose a simple algorithm for updating to PEs and claim it has advantages over classical probabilistic inference in biologically relevant scenarios: low data, limited algorithmic resources. Our setting is a learner attempting to infer a single state from ambiguous observations over repeated trials. For concreteness, we consider the state as a reward rt∗\begin{document}$r_{t}^{*}$\end{document} associated with that state. The received reward at time t, rt, takes the form:
(2a)
rt=rt∗+Cm(zt) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{t}=r_{t}^{*}+C_{m}\left(z_{t}\right) \tag{(2a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(2b)
rt+1∗−rt∗=Cp(zt), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{t+1}^{*}-r_{t}^{*}=C_{p}\left(z_{t}\right), \tag{(2b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This models a general situation where large-world causes of systematic change (volatility, Cp(zt)) and unsystematic, noisy fluctuations (stochasticity, Cm(zt)) both affect rt. The learner holds a state estimate r^t\begin{document}$\hat{r}_{t}$\end{document}, with estimation error: et=rt∗−r^t\begin{document}$e_{t}=r_{t}^{*}-\hat{r}_{t}$\end{document}. This estimate is updated from prediction errors: δt+1=rt+1−r^t\begin{document}$\delta_{t+1}=r_{t+1}-\hat{r}_{t}$\end{document}.
r^t+1−r^t⏟change in estimate=αt⏟learning rate×δt+1⏟PE \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \underbrace{\hat{r}_{t+1}-\hat{r}_{t}}_{\text {change in estimate }}=\underbrace{\alpha_{t}}_{\text {learning rate }} \times \underbrace{\delta_{t+1}}_{\text {PE }}$$\end{document}
An excessive learning rate (αt ≈1) erases valuable accumulated experience by overreacting to stochastic fluctuations. An insufficient learning rate (αt ≈ 0) cannot keep up with systematic volatility, or reduce a large estimation error. The truly optimal learning rate (zero estimation error on trial t + 1, see SI S1) is
(3)
αt∗=Cp(zt)+etCm(zt+1)+Cp(zt)+et. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \alpha_{t}^{*}=\frac{C_{p}\left(z_{t}\right)+e_{t}}{C_{m}\left(z_{t+1}\right)+C_{p}\left(z_{t}\right)+e_{t}} . \tag{(3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This learning rate in inaccessible to any learning algorithm: Cm, Cp, and et will fluctuate on each timestep, and their values cannot be disambiguated from the scalar PE signal which sums their individual effects.

We formulate a simple Hetlearn strategy for PE updates: Take n parallel sublearners, each with their own estimates: {r^t(1),…,r^t(n)}\begin{document}$\left\{\hat{r}_{t}^{(1)}, \ldots, \hat{r}_{t}^{(n)}\right\}$\end{document}. The ith learner receives a prediction error δt(i)\begin{document}$\delta_{t}^{(i)}$\end{document}, and updates its estimate as:
r^t+1(i)=r^t(i)+α(i)δt(i) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \hat{r}_{t+1}^{(i)}=\hat{r}_{t}^{(i)}+\alpha^{(i)} \delta_{t}^{(i)}$$\end{document}
where α(i) is fixed and heterogeneous across sublearners. The overall prediction is a weighted vote: r^t=∑i=1nr^t(i)wt(i)\begin{document}$\hat{r}_{t}=\sum_{i=1}^{n} \hat{r}_{t}^{(i)} w_{t}^{(i)}$\end{document}, where wt(i)\begin{document}$w_{t}^{(i)}$\end{document} is the weight assigned to the ith sublearner.

To determine the weights, the agent uses an estimate of recent error for each sublearner. One simple estimate is:
e^t+1(i)=τ‖δt+1(i)‖22+(1−τ)e^t(i): \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \hat{e}_{t+1}^{(i)}=\tau\left\|\delta_{t+1}^{(i)}\right\|_{2}^{2}+(1-\tau) \hat{e}_{t}^{(i)}:$$\end{document}
a moving average of PE magnitude, with recency bias 0 ≤ τ ≤ 1. Weights are then wt(i)∝1e^t(i)\begin{document}$w_{t}^{(i)} \propto \frac{1}{\hat{e}_{t}^{(i)}}$\end{document}, normalised so that∑iwt(i)=1\begin{document}$\sum_{i} w_{t}^{(i)}=1$\end{document}. The free parameters in this strategy are the learning rates α(i) of each sublearner, and the recency bias τ .

Hetlearn differs importantly from probabilistic inference, where large-world effects must be modelled as probability distributions to infer learning rate (Eq. (1)). We now demonstrate this on a simple didactic example, where we compare Hetlearn to the Kalman Filter (KF): a popular model for how animals learn from prediction errors [11, 25, 20], with biologically plausible neural circuit implementations [12, 54]. The KF assumes fixed Gaussian distributions:
(4a)
Cm(zt)∼N(0,σm2)(stochasticity) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle C_m\left(z_t\right) \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, \sigma_m^2\right) \quad \text { (stochasticity) }$$\end{document}
(4b)
Cp(zt)∼N(0,σp2)(volatility) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle C_p\left(z_t\right) \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, \sigma_p^2\right) \quad \text { (volatility) }$$\end{document}
(4c)
et∼N(0,ct)(estimation error). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle e_{t} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, c_{t}\right) \quad \text { (estimation error). }$$\end{document}
With these assumptions, baseline reward is a Gaussian distribution whose mean updates with a dynamic, Bayes-optimal learning rate. The learning rate calculation, however, requires knowledge of σm2,σp2\begin{document}$\sigma_{m}^{2}, \sigma_{p}^{2}$\end{document}, and c0 as part of its generative model (Methods, (7)). We can say that these form part of the fixed ‘small-world’ structure assumed by the model.

Our model learns to predict the current xt by adapting a matrix A representing linear, recurrent Kenyoncell connections. This adaptation follows the simple online-least-mean-squares rule, which can be implemented with Hebbian plasticity [53]:
δt=xt+1−AxtAt+1=At+αxtδtT. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & \delta_{t}=x_{t+1}-A x_{t} \\ & A_{t+1}=A_{t}+\alpha x_{t} \delta_{t}^{T} .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
We use the learning rate α = 0.4. Biologically, structural changes in the calyx, presynaptic to Kenyon cells, do accompany the consolidation of olfactory conditioned memories [10, 4].

On each stimulus presentation, the system makes a prediction ŷt+1 of the next unconditioned stimuli. These are later compared against a vector of experienced unconditioned stimuli yt, represented in separate circuits such as the lateral horn. The prediction sums engram activations:
y^t+1=∑is(i)⟨e(i),xt⟩v(i) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \hat{y}_{t+1}=\sum_{i} s^{(i)}\left\langle e^{(i)}, x_{t}\right\rangle v^{(i)}$$\end{document}
If the PE ∥yt+1 − ŷt+1∥2 exceeds a threshold (> T1 = 0.3) and all extant engram activations ⟨e(i), xt⟩ are low (< T2 = 0.3), then a new engram is formed with template xt. If the PE was an unsystematic fluke, this engram will not contribute constructively to future predictions and will die (described later). If not, it will crystallise without interfering with different existing engrams.

Engram strengths adapt to each PE. To minimise interference of each adaptation with different conditioned responses, engrams compete to claim and adapt to errors, a process we call belief competition. We take belief as
(6)
bt(i)=σ(⟨e(i),xt⟩), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} b_{t}^{(i)}=\sigma\left(\left\langle e^{(i)}, x_{t}\right\rangle\right), \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where σ(x)i=e−βxi∑je−βxj\begin{document}$\sigma(x)_{i}=\frac{e^{-\beta x_{i}}}{\sum_{j} e^{-\beta x_{j}}}$\end{document}is the softmax function. We took β = 10. Strengths then update as
sf(i)→sf(i)+(αfbt(i)v(i))(yt−y^t)ss(i)→ss(i)+(αsbt(i)v(i))(yt−y^t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & s_{f}^{(i)} \rightarrow s_{f}^{(i)}+\left(\alpha_{f} b_{t}^{(i)} v^{(i)}\right)\left(y_{t}-\hat{y}_{t}\right) \\ & s_{s}^{(i)} \rightarrow s_{s}^{(i)}+\left(\alpha_{s} b_{t}^{(i)} v^{(i)}\right)\left(y_{t}-\hat{y}_{t}\right) .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
Slow strengths only update once the fast strength has crossed a threshold (Tf = 0.4) replicating the repeated conditioniong required to form engrams in slow-learning MBON compartments such as α1. If a fast strength dips below 0.1 before a slow strength has activated, the engram is extinguished.

In reinforcement learning, the value of an environmental state is the long-term predicted reward, which incorporates the reward obtained at future states likely to be visited. Correspondingly, our model values a KC representation xt by evolving the representation through the recurrent matrix A, which predicts future representationas as x^t+j=Ajxt\begin{document}$\hat{x}_{t+j}=A^{j} x_{t}$\end{document}. The synaptic output of these dynamics are integrated over time against different engrams, determining the engram-specific value contribution. Mathematically,
V(i)(xt)=∑j=0∞ss(i)⟨e(i),Ajxt⟩ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle V^{(i)}\left(x_{t}\right)=\sum_{j=0}^{\infty} s_{s}^{(i)}\left\langle e^{(i)}, A^{j} x_{t}\right\rangle$$\end{document}
with overall value V(xt)=∑iV(i)(xt)\begin{document}$V\left(x_{t}\right)=\sum_{i} V^{(i)}\left(x_{t}\right)$\end{document}. These dynamics soon die out biologically, and in the model (as ∥Aj+1∥ < ∥Aj ∥), so we terminate the integration at j = 3.

The Kalman Filter updates valences from observed PEs δt according to the following equations:
(7a)
vt+1=vt+αtδt+1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} v_{t+1}=v_{t}+\alpha_{t} \delta_{t+1} \tag{(7a} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(7b)
ct+1=(1−αt)(ct+σp,t2) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} c_{t+1}=\left(1-\alpha_{t}\right)\left(c_{t}+\sigma_{p, t}^{2}\right) \tag{7b} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(7c)
αt=ct+σp,t2ct+σp,t2+σm,t2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \alpha_{t}=\frac{c_{t}+\sigma_{p, t}^{2}}{c_{t}+\sigma_{p, t}^{2}+\sigma_{m, t}^{2}} . \tag{(7c} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This requires seeding with c1=E[(r1∗−v1)2]\begin{document}$c_{1}=\mathbb{E}\left[\left(r_{1}^{*}-v_{1}\right)^{2}\right]$\end{document}: the initial covariance. Although ethologically inaccessible, we seeded all our simulations with the correct value of c1, giving an unfair advantage over Hetlearn.

The ALS method builds estimates σ^m,t2,σ^p,t2\begin{document}$\hat{\sigma}_{m, t}^{2}, \hat{\sigma}_{p, t}^{2}$\end{document} by solving the following regression problem:
(8a)
A[σ^p,t2σ^m,t2]=b,where \begin{document}$$\displaystyle A\left[\begin{array}{c} \hat{\sigma}_{p, t}^{2} \tag{$(8 \mathrm{a}$}\\ \hat{\sigma}_{m, t}^{2} \end{array}\right]=b, \text { where }$$\end{document}
(8b)
Aj,∙=(1−α)j−1α(2−α)[1−α−α2] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle A_{j, \bullet}=\frac{(1-\alpha)^{j-1}}{\alpha(2-\alpha)}\left[\begin{array}{ll} 1-\alpha & -\alpha^{2} \tag{8b} \end{array}\right]$$\end{document}
(8c)
bj=1t−j∑t′=1t−jYt+jYt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} b_{j}=\frac{1}{t-j} \sum_{t^{\prime}=1}^{t-j} \mathcal{Y}_{t+j} \mathcal{Y}_{t} . \tag{( 8 c} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Here, Aj,• represents the jth row of A, and bj represents the sample estimate of E [Yt+jYt].

Private estimates of stochasticity and volatility update stochastically and identically. For volatility, the update equation is:
(σp,t2)−1=ηp−1ϵt(σp,t−12)−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left(\sigma_{p, t}^{2}\right)^{-1}=\eta_{p}^{-1} \epsilon_{t}\left(\sigma_{p, t-1}^{2}\right)^{-1}$$\end{document}
where

The ith particle calculates probabilities for the next reward by assuming
(9)
rt∼N(v^ti,c^ti+σp,t2i+σm,t2i). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle r_t \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\hat{v}_t^i, \hat{c}_t^i+\sigma_{p, t}^2{ }^i+\sigma_{m, t}^2{ }^i\right) .$$\end{document}
When an actual reward arrives, the relative weights of the particles are taken as
wti∝P[rt∣v^ti] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle w_{t}^{i} \propto \mathbb{P}\left[r_{t} \mid \hat{v}_{t}^{i}\right]$$\end{document}
So particles whose probabilistic model predicted the actual reward with higher probability are weighted more. The weights are then normalised so that ∑iwti=1\begin{document}$\sum_{i} w_{t}^{i}=1$\end{document}. Thus the weights represent a probability distribution over particles: ℙp, where the probability of the ith particle is wi. The state of the overall particle collection is the expectation over this distribution:
(10)
E[v^t]=∑i=1Nwtiv^ti. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbb{E}\left[\hat{v}_{t}\right]=\sum_{i=1}^{N} w_{t}^{i} \hat{v}_{t}^{i} . \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We follow [42] in using the Systematic Resampling procedure, to resample particles (deleting low-probability particles and duplicating high-probability particles) if their weight inequality exceeds 0.5.
